A||||12||burdell1||Restoring the Interior
Z||000000||burdell1||07-05-99||10:34 AM||||Does anyone having any tips on restoring the interior? I have an '85 GT with the light grey interior which doesn't look as new as I want. Has anyone tried to reletter everything so it looks new? Even if I were to get new parts, the letters wear off eventually.
Z||000001||Eric||07-05-99||05:20 PM||||What do you mean by lettering?  Are you wanting some information on repainting?  If that's the case, I've done it.  It's really easy to paint the plastic interior parts as long as you use plastic paint.  Headliner is a bit more work, but if you take your time and do it carefully, it can look quite good.  
Z||000002||Harry||07-05-99||05:35 PM||||If your talking about the dash pieces you can buy new ones from the Fiero #$%^ the have a new catalog out with more stuff for the dash ie dimmer controls etc etc
Z||000003||Andy||07-05-99||06:44 PM||||When I bought my 87 Gt,the interior was in bad shape.I needed a stering colume,so before I decided to by a new one[tilt stering] I went to a local junk yard and found an 87 with good interior,good the hole inside for $175.Some of it was kinde faded and dirty.The speaker covers had faded to a blue,suppose to be gray.I bought some Vinyl+Plasitc color from SEM {automotive paint center]and repainted them,they turned out great,also redone the steringwheel,but before you repaint anything make sure you clean it with Acetone.It wont hurt anything,even the Vinyl.It drys pretty fast.I used it on the doorpanels and then wiped them down with Armorall.For the carpets if there are not torne take them out and take'm to a carwash and hose'm off.I sprayed mine down with some purple power just before I washed them.The force off the water get the dirt out well,how ever I wouldn't do it if the carpets are worne out or torne.It took the carpets about 3 days to dry 
Z||000004||lowCG||07-06-99||12:53 AM||||Just got this car with about a cup or two of mildew-water under each carpet,threw away the fouled pad/sound deadening stuff,found this anti fungus version of the Fantastic cleaner that doesn't use bleach,got a bottle and just swamped the car with it,scrubbed,vacumed,now waiting for it to dry in a day or two.We'll see if that killed off the microbes lurking in my carpets,and removed the smells.<br>Does anyone know where to get new rubber for where the door meets the window?<br>
Z||000005||burdell1||07-06-99||08:15 AM||||When I say "lettering" I am talking about the letters on say the power windows buttons or the light switch. I could just buy new ones, but they will eventually rub off and I've been putting A LOT of money in my car lately just to have it run.  I figure if I have the letters painted with a certain paint, they won't rub off, but I am not sure what I should use (or how to make it look professional.)<br>
Z||000006||Eric||07-06-99||03:36 PM||||Get some white plastic paint.  I think the kind a a hobby store would work.  Also get a tiny little paint brush when you are there.  Take your time.  If your lettering is like mine, you can still see where it should be.  <p>LowCG, when you took your carpet out how much did it weigh.  Would it be worth it, when doing a carpet replacement, to take out the original carpet and rubber backing and replace the backing with a lightweight foam?
Z||000007||theogre||07-06-99||07:58 PM||||The lettering on GM controls, is for the most part, simply painted on, printed, or pressure transfer. None of those methods last well.<p>Only thermal bond, or injection, lettering lasts on high use plastic surfaces. ((Computer Keyboards have this same problem.))<p>If the lettering is not a filled engraving, which is pretty easy to repaint, then expect any thing you use to be temporary. Filled engravings are things like the mark on the heater/AC fan speed knob which is usually paint in a depressed spot on the knob. The depression serves to protect the paint from full contact with the user. Switches like the defoger, trunk latch, and headlights, are just paint on flat surfaces, and that always wears fast.
Z||000008||DaRkLoRD||07-06-99||08:53 PM||||Just had a crazy thought, I don't know if this would work or not, but I thought I'd share it anyway.<p>What about taking a small engraving tool, possibly on a dremel or something, and going over the letters.. then smooth any really rough edges, fill with paint, and put something clear on top so it's flush with the panel/button/whatever.. clear nail polish or some type of sealant..<p>comments?
Z||000009||DaRkLoRD||07-06-99||08:58 PM||||Speaking of the Fiero interior, does anyone have any links for replacing parts of the interior? I've seen one website about an amazing stereo install (URL was posted here before) and a totally new dash and center console was fabricated for the Fiero.<p>Anyone know any more links like this?<p>a walk-thru of the Fiero disassembly would be amazing.. never seen one on the net tho. :)
Z||000010||SCCA FIERO||07-06-99||10:48 PM||||If you want a different dash/interior go here <A HREF="http://www.cybercars.com/mnfr/fierohq/war.htm" TARGET=_blank>http://www.cybercars.com/mnfr/fierohq/war.htm</A>  If you want to find replacement parts for the stock interior go here <A HREF="http://www.fierostore.com/" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fierostore.com/</A>  There are other places to find OEM peices and aftermarket peices just surf around.<p>I am the one that posted the picture of the stereo in that Fiero. The guy that owns the Fiero made that dash and interior.<p>I have torn out the interior in a few Fieros. It ain't hard.
Z||000011||burdell1||07-06-99||11:55 PM||||has anybody tried putting something over the lettering (like a clearcoat) so it will last? How do the letters look in your cars?
Z||000012||batboy||07-07-99||06:32 AM||||My Fiero's interior shows some signs of wear after over 12 years of use. I never considered relettering because I don't have a steady enough hand or good enough penmanship. You would think that by putting in a new interior, it ought to last at least another 12 years or more if you take care of it. Anything a guy can do to improve the looks of the interior is time well spent. When you take a babe for a ride, the interior is what she sees. As for the clear coat, it might work, maybe try it on an extra similar part first to see if you like how it looks.
